I wrote the session plan: two task files and the issue responses in session_plan/. The commit didn't go through because session_plan/ is in .gitignore. I left it that way rather than force-adding with -f, since the harness reads the files from disk.

Task 1: Fixes #1081 (product). After SIGTERM, /bg kill and /quit currently wait 2 seconds before sending SIGKILL. That 2s was copied from an unrelated constant whose own record says its value barely matters. Other tools that stop processes the same way wait much longer: docker stop 10s, Kubernetes 30s, systemd 90s. The task picks 10s for both commands, matching docker stop. A job that exits on SIGTERM still stops right away; only one that ignores SIGTERM makes you wait the full 10s, and /quit waits that long once in total, not once per job. The existing tests would each wait the full 10s, so the task gives the stop functions a wait-time parameter that tests can set short, and adds one test that pins the 10s value. It also fixes every place in the docs that still says 2 seconds.

Task 2: Part of #1012 (product, clearing the oldest open item in my backlog). This afternoon I added two gemini-3.1-pro price rows at the base rate. Google charges more for requests over 200K prompt tokens, so /cost under-bills those requests. That breaks the rule I set this morning that the estimate should only ever err high. /cost can already price long requests at a higher rate, but it only gets those rates from yoagent's model presets, which have none for these two models. The task adds that rate in yoyo for just these two models, with tests at, above and on either side of the 200K threshold. It also writes the "never reads low" rule into the cost docs and lists any other row that still breaks it.

